---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

const guard = `let run = mcpd.trace("support agent");
{
    let step = run.child_of("retrieve", SpanKind::Retrieval);
    step.set_input("order 5512");
    // Whatever this step does. The span ends at the closing brace, and it ends
    // on an early return, on a ? and on a panic as well.
}
run.end();`

const taskLocal = `// Opening a span does not make it current. scope() does.
let run = mcpd.trace("support agent");
run.scope(async {
    lookup(&mcpd).await;
})
.await;
run.end();

async fn lookup(mcpd: &Mcpd) {
    // No parent was threaded into this function, and this still nests under
    // the run, because the task-local carries it.
    let step = mcpd.span("lookup_order", SpanKind::ToolCall);
    step.set_output(serde_json::json!({ "status": "shipped" }));
}`

const propagation = `// Outbound: put the header on the request.
let header = span.traceparent();

// Inbound: continue the caller's trace instead of starting a new one. An
// absent or malformed header starts a fresh trace rather than failing.
let run = mcpd.trace_from("charge card", inbound_header, SpanKind::AgentRun);`

const payload = `generation.set_model("claude-sonnet-4", "anthropic");
generation.set_usage(TokenUsage {
    input_tokens: Some(412),
    output_tokens: Some(96),
    ..TokenUsage::default()
});
// Or, when the breakdown does not matter:
generation.set_tokens(412, 96);

// How the model was called. Settings, not payload: kept with capture off.
generation.set_model_parameters(ModelParameters {
    temperature: Some(0.0),
    seed: Some(0),
    max_tokens: Some(1024),
    ..ModelParameters::default()
});

// A message is required. "Something failed" is not actionable.
tool.set_error("sms gateway timed out after 5s");

// Or record the error you are already holding, which reads its message and kind.
tool.record_error(&err);`

const shutdown = `#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let mcpd = Mcpd::new();

    // ... the run ...

    // Ignoring this is a reasonable default: an observability failure is not
    // an application failure. Logging it costs one line.
    if let Err(e) = mcpd.shutdown().await {
        eprintln!("mcpd could not export: {e}");
    }
}`
---

<Doc
  title="Rust"
  description="The mcpd SDK for Rust: an RAII span guard that ends on drop, a tokio task-local for context, and instrumentation that returns no errors and panics on nothing."
  lede="A span is a guard that ends when it is dropped, so an early return or an error cannot leave one open. Context travels in a tokio task-local."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    The SDK exports on a background tokio task, so a runtime is what it expects
    to find. It runs without one, and then nothing is sent until you call
    <code>flush()</code>, which is the right behaviour for a synchronous tool but
    not what a long-lived service wants.
  </p>

  <Command
    command="cargo add mcpd-sdk"
    primaryLabel="The SDK"
    secondary={[{ label: 'A runtime, if the crate has none', command: 'cargo add tokio --features macros,rt-multi-thread' }]}
  />

  <h2>Construct the client</h2>
  <p>
    One client per process. <code>Mcpd::new()</code> reads the environment and
    talks to a local mcpd with nothing set at all. Construction never fails: a
    configuration mcpd cannot use disables the client and logs why, because an
    observability call is not a thing a program should have to handle an error
    from.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['rust']} region="init" title="connect" />

  <p>Anything set on the builder after <code>Config::from_env()</code> wins over the environment.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Environment</th><th>Default</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>endpoint</code></td><td><code>MCPD_ENDPOINT</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:4317</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>api_key</code></td><td><code>MCPD_API_KEY</code></td><td>none</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>project</code></td><td><code>MCPD_PROJECT</code></td><td><code>default</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>environment</code></td><td><code>MCPD_ENVIRONMENT</code></td><td>none</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service_name</code></td><td><code>MCPD_SERVICE_NAME</code></td><td>none</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service_version</code></td><td><code>MCPD_SERVICE_VERSION</code></td><td>none</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sample</code></td><td><code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code></td><td><code>1.0</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>batch_size</code></td><td><code>MCPD_BATCH_SIZE</code></td><td><code>128</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>flush_interval</code></td><td><code>MCPD_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS</code></td><td><code>2s</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>max_queue</code></td><td><code>MCPD_MAX_QUEUE</code></td><td><code>8192</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td><code>MCPD_TIMEOUT_MS</code></td><td><code>10s</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>enabled</code></td><td><code>MCPD_ENABLED</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>capture_payloads</code></td><td><code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>log_level</code></td><td><code>MCPD_LOG_LEVEL</code></td><td><code>debug</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A setting that will not parse is skipped with a warning and the default is
    used, because a typo in a deployment's environment must not stop the program.
    <code>enabled = false</code> makes every call a no-op that still returns
    valid objects; ids are still well formed and <code>traceparent</code> still
    propagates, so a test can turn mcpd off without touching the code under test.
  </p>

  <h2>Record a run</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Span</code> is an RAII guard. It ends when it is dropped, which is the
    scoped form in Rust: a block, a scope, or the end of the function. Calling
    <code>end()</code> yourself does the same thing, and the guard is there so
    that an early return or a panic cannot leave a span open and unfinished.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="a span is a guard"
    samples={[{ lang: 'rust', label: 'Rust', shiki: 'rust', code: guard }]}
  />

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['rust']} region="trace" title="record a run" />

  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Inside a process, context travels in a tokio task-local. Holding a span does
    not make it current: <code>Span::scope</code> sets the task-local for a
    future and <code>Span::sync_scope</code> does it for a closure. Inside either,
    <code>Mcpd::span</code> attaches to the run in progress, so a helper several
    layers down nests correctly without being handed the parent.
    <code>Span::child_of</code> is the explicit alternative when you are already
    holding the parent.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="the task-local"
    samples={[{ lang: 'rust', label: 'Rust', shiki: 'rust', code: taskLocal }]}
  />

  <p>
    Between processes, context travels in a W3C <code>traceparent</code> header.
    The sampling flag rides along with it, so the two halves of a trace agree
    about whether the trace is being kept.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="across a service boundary"
    samples={[{ lang: 'rust', label: 'Rust', shiki: 'rust', code: propagation }]}
  />

  <h2>Model, usage and errors</h2>
  <p>
    Token counts are what mcpd prices at ingest, so the cost on the trace is the
    cost you were billed. Recording an error sets the span's status to
    <code>error</code> in the same move, so the two can never disagree, and the
    message is a required argument rather than an option.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>set_model_parameters</code> records the settings the call was made
    with: <code>temperature</code>, <code>top_p</code>, <code>top_k</code>,
    <code>max_tokens</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>stop_sequences</code> and
    <code>has_response_format</code>. They are settings rather than payload, so
    they survive <code>with_capture_payloads(false)</code>, which is the case
    that matters: they are what somebody reproducing the run has to match.
    <code>None</code> is absent and <code>Some(0)</code> is a value, so a zero
    seed is a seed. The schema behind <code>has_response_format</code> is not
    recorded, only whether one was in force.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="what a span carries"
    samples={[{ lang: 'rust', label: 'Rust', shiki: 'rust', code: payload }]}
  />

  <p>
    The rest of the setters are <code>set_input</code>, <code>set_output</code>,
    <code>set_attributes</code>, <code>set_metadata</code>,
    <code>set_tags</code>, <code>set_entity</code>, <code>set_session</code>,
    <code>set_user</code>, <code>set_status</code> and
    <code>set_error_detail</code>. All of them take <code>&amp;self</code>, so a
    span can be filled in from anywhere it is borrowed.
  </p>

  <h2>Payloads, sinks and finished spans</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Config::with_capture_payloads(false)</code> keeps prompts and
    completions in the process: spans still carry their timing, status, usage,
    model, model parameters, correlation and errors, and <code>input</code> and
    <code>output</code> are dropped before they reach the queue. A span
    overrides the client for itself with <code>span.capture_payloads(..)</code>,
    in either direction.
  </p>
  <p>
    The exporter hands every batch to the client's sink. The HTTP transport is
    the default; <code>Config::with_sink</code> takes a
    <code>RecordingSink</code>, which keeps batches in memory so a test can
    assert what was recorded without a server, or a <code>ConsoleSink</code>,
    which prints one JSON line per signal. A sink that fails is a failed
    export, reported once per interval and never raised.
    <code>mcpd.stats()</code> answers <code>queued</code>, <code>dropped</code>,
    <code>capacity</code>, <code>exported</code>, <code>failed</code> and
    <code>rejected</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd.record(span)</code> queues a span that already ended, ids,
    parent, start and end included, as an adapter or an import holds it: never
    re-parented, re-timed or re-sampled. A malformed record is dropped and
    counted as <code>rejected</code>. <code>span.set_external_parent</code>
    carries a parent from a tracing system that is not mcpd, for correlation
    only.
  </p>

  <h2>Durable runs</h2>
  <p>
    The same client serves the daemon's durable runs. <code>Workflow::new</code>
    builds the definition and keeps the step bodies as async closures; a
    <code>Worker</code> registers it, long-polls for leases and serves each one
    on a task, and <code>start_run</code>, <code>watch_run</code>,
    <code>publish_event</code> and the rest mirror the run surface one for
    one. A body returns <code>StepFailure::retryable</code> to be retried
    within the step's budget, and records spans under its step through the
    <code>StepContext</code>. Every span opened that way, and every span nested
    under one, adds its usage to the step when it ends, so the daemon prices
    what the trace shows without the body summing tokens by hand;
    <code>set_usage</code> is the explicit override. The builder's
    <code>on_lease</code>, <code>on_step_start</code>, <code>on_step_end</code>
    and <code>on_conflict</code> hooks and <code>worker.stats()</code> make the
    worker observable from inside.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['rust']} example="workflow" region="workflow" title="a workflow" />
  <p>
    What each construct does, the lease protocol and the run API are on
    <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a>,
    <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">Workers</a> and
    <a href="/docs/runs" class="link">Runs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scores and feedback</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>Scale</code> is a required argument, not an option. A bare 0.9 with no
    declared range cannot be compared to anything, and by the time someone is
    reading the chart it is too late to ask what it meant.
    <code>Scale::UNIT</code> is the usual one, 0.0 to 1.0. Feedback is a person's
    judgement, kept apart from a score because the two answer different questions
    and must never be averaged together.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['rust']} region="score" title="score and feedback" />

  <h2>Flush and shutdown</h2>
  <p>
    Recording appends to an in-memory queue and returns. <code>flush()</code>
    sends what is queued; <code>shutdown()</code> flushes, stops the worker and is
    safe to call twice. Both are awaitable, and both return the last export
    failure so you can log it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A short-lived process must call one of them. A binary that exits without
    shutting down exits with its spans still in memory, and in a serverless
    function the runtime freezes the process the moment the handler returns, so
    the background task never gets another turn.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="shutting down"
    samples={[{ lang: 'rust', label: 'Rust', shiki: 'rust', code: shutdown }]}
  />

  <p>
    Signals lost to a failed flush are not re-queued. The transport has already
    retried, and a queue that refills itself on failure is how an outage becomes
    an out-of-memory kill.
  </p>

  <h2>What you can rely on</h2>
  <p>
    With the daemon stopped, the example runs to completion, prints what it could
    not do, and exits zero.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="with no daemon running"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'cargo run -p mcpd-sdk --example quickstart' },
      { out: '    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo]' },
      { out: '     Running `target/debug/examples/quickstart`' },
      { out: 'mcpd could not export: export failed: mcpd could not be reached', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: 'could not read the trace back: mcpd could not be reached', tone: 'warn' },
      { cmd: 'echo $?' },
      { out: '0', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />

  <ul>
    <li>
      No instrumentation call returns an error or panics. An unreachable mcpd, a
      serialisation failure or a full queue is logged through
      <code>tracing</code> at most once per flush interval and swallowed.
      Observability must never take down the thing it observes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Recording never blocks. Export happens on a background tokio task, so your
      agent's latency does not depend on mcpd being fast, or up.
    </li>
    <li>
      The queue is capped at <code>max_queue</code>. When it is full the oldest
      signals are discarded and <code>mcpd.stats().dropped</code> counts them.
      Dropping the oldest is a smaller failure than an out-of-memory kill.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sampling below 1.0 is deterministic on the trace id rather than random, so
      every process in a fleet makes the same decision about the same trace and a
      trace that crosses a service boundary is never half recorded.
    </li>
    <li>
      A span is exported when it starts and updated when it ends, so a run that
      crashes mid-flight is still readable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reading it back</h2>
  <p>
    The same client reads. Every method mirrors one HTTP endpoint and returns the
    generated types, which come from the specification by way of
    <code>mcpd-core</code> and are re-exported as <code>mcpd_sdk::model</code>.
    Reads return a <code>Result</code>, because a query you awaited and cannot
    answer is news.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['rust']} region="query" title="search and analyse" />

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/searching" class="link">Finding a run</a> covers the filters
    in full. <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">The data model</a>
    lists every field these types carry.
  </p>
</Doc>
